<spell-notebook "toast usage">
	<header level="1">
		Toast
		<header>A toast allows users to receive a short, non-blocking notification.</>
	</>
	<toast-tab-bar showing="usage"/>
	<section>
		<menu class="ui-docs-section-tabs" appearance="segmented" aria-label="Sections">
			<item link="#using-it">Using it</>
			<item link="#closing">Closing</>
			<item link="#events">Events</>
			<item link="#keyboard">Keyboard</>
			<item link="#accessibility">Accessibility</>
			<item link="#framework-usage">Framework usage</>
		</>
	</>

	<!-- USAGE:  how to use it (attributes in practice, events, framework snippets), keyboard, accessibility. -->
	<section label="Using it">
		<markdown>
			Two ways to show a toast:

			- `UI.toast({...})` builds a `<ui-toast>` in a container per `position` (`bottom right` by default;
			  `top right`, `top left`, `top center`, `bottom left`, `bottom center`, `centered`), in the top
			  layer, and removes it once it has closed. Its options are Fomantic's `$.toast()` settings
			  (`title`, `message`, `class`, `displayTime` (3000 by default), `showIcon` (the type's own by default), `showProgress`,
			  `progressUp`, `closeIcon`, `closeOnClick`, `pauseOnHover`, `compact`, `actions`, `classActions`,
			  `newestOnTop`, `horizontal`). Text is always set as text, never parsed as HTML.
			- A `<ui-toast>` written in the page shows where it is, like any element, and stays until closed
			  unless it has a `display-time` (milliseconds, or `auto`: about as long as it takes to read).

			`UI.toast()` returns `{ id, closed, element }`: `closed` resolves when the toast is gone, and
			`UI.toasts.dismiss(id)` closes it early. It exists once the toast family has loaded: import
			`spell/ui/ui-toast`, or use a `<ui-toast>` somewhere under `<ui-root>`.

			Every `class` word also stays on the `<ui-toast>`, so one toast can be themed on its own:
			`UI.toast({ class: "ready", ... })` with `ui-toast.ready { --ui-toast-background: ...;
			--ui-toast-font-size: 14px }`.
		</>
		<markdown>
			```js
			import { UI } from "spell/ui/core"
			import "spell/ui/ui-toast" // registers UI.toast()

			await UI.load()
			const toast = UI.toast({ message: "Moved to archive", displayTime: 5000, actions: [{ text: "Undo", click: undo }] })
			await toast.closed // gone:  countdown, click, action ...
			```
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Closing">
		<markdown>
			What closes a toast: the countdown, the close icon, a click on it (`close-on-click`, off with a
			close icon, actions or form controls inside), Escape while focus is inside, an action, a `--close`
			invoker command, or `host.close()`. Each goes through the cancelable `ui-closing` (`detail.reason`),
			then the exit animation, then `hidden` on the host and `ui-closed`.

			A `<ui-button commandfor="id" command="--close">` (or a native button) closes a toast in the page
			with no script. Only `--close`: a closed toast stays closed (`hidden`, never removed), so there is
			nothing for `--show` or `--toggle` to bring back; insert a new toast instead.

			The countdown pauses while the pointer is over the toast (`pause-on-hover`) and always while focus
			is inside it. `progress="top"` or `"bottom"` shows it as a bar, which keeps running under reduced
			motion: it tells time, it isn't decoration.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Events">
		<markdown>
			- `ui-opened` (`{ displayTime }`): shown, its entry animation finished; the countdown has started.
			- `ui-closing` (`{ reason }`, cancelable): about to close; `preventDefault()` keeps it.
			- `ui-closed` (`{ reason }`): closed, its exit animation finished; the host is `hidden` now.
			- `ui-approving` / `ui-denying` (`{ action }`, cancelable): an approve action (`positive`, `.approve`,
			  `.ok`) or a deny one (`negative`, `.deny`, `.cancel`) was activated; `preventDefault()` keeps
			  the toast open.
		</>
		<markdown>
			```js
			document.querySelector("#saved").addEventListener("ui-closing", (event) => {
			  if (event.detail.reason === "timeout" && stillSaving) event.preventDefault() // keep it up
			})
			```
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Keyboard">
		<markdown>
			A toast never takes focus.

			- **Tab** reaches its close icon and actions; focus inside pauses the countdown.
			- **Escape** closes the toast while focus is inside it.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Accessibility">
		<markdown>
			- A toast is a `role="status"` live region, so it's announced without moving focus;
			  `type="error"` makes it a `role="alert"`, announced at once.
			- Keep toasts for news that can be missed. Anything the user must act on belongs in a
			  [modal](/components/ui/ui-modal.html) or on the page: a toast with `displayTime: 0` and actions is a
			  compromise.
			- The toast containers of `UI.toast()` are `region` landmarks named "Notifications" (translated).
			- KNOWN LIMIT: while a modal `<dialog>` is open, everything outside it is inert, toast containers
			  included. A toast shown then still counts down, but can't be clicked, focused or announced.
			- If the element fails to render, it is no longer a toast: only children written inside it still show,
			  through a bare `<slot>`, unannounced, with no close button, `display-time` or actions.
		</>
	</>

	<section label="Framework usage">
		<markdown>
			Most apps call `UI.toast()` from an event handler. A `<ui-toast>` in a template is an ordinary
			element: listen for `ui-closed` to drop it from your state.
		</>
		<header level="4" id="framework-react">React 19</>
		<markdown>
			```jsx
			{saved && <ui-toast type="success" message="Saved" display-time="3000" onui-hide={() => setSaved(false)} />}
			```
		</>
		<header level="4" id="framework-vue">Vue 3.5</>
		<markdown>
			```html
			<!-- compilerOptions.isCustomElement = (tag) => tag.startsWith("ui-") -->
			<ui-toast v-if="saved" type="success" message="Saved" display-time="3000" @ui-closed="saved = false" />
			```
		</>
		<header level="4" id="framework-solid">Solid 2</>
		<markdown>
			```jsx
			// events and rich data are plain props:  `on-<event>={...}`, `options={...}`
			<Show when={saved()}>
			  <ui-toast
			    type="success"
			    message="Saved"
			    display-time="3000"
			    on-ui-closed={() => setSaved(false)}
			  />
			</Show>
			```
		</>
	</>
</spell-notebook>
